# docker/.env.example — the local environment template for
# srn://metaframework/environment/compose.
#
#     cp docker/.env.example docker/.env
#
# and edit that copy. `docker/.env` is gitignored (`.env*` in /.gitignore, with
# `!.env.example` as the one exception), and this repository is PUBLIC.
#
# NO VALUE IN THIS FILE IS A SECRET, and none ever may be:
# deployment-files-live-under-docker AC-3 — "No secret value appears in any file
# under docker/. Templates name keys and say where the value comes from." A
# default committed here is how a development secret reaches production, so
# every secret key below is empty and stays empty.
#
# Compose loads docker/.env automatically because it sits beside
# docker/compose.yaml, so everything in PART ONE takes effect with no flag.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART ONE — knobs docker/compose.yaml reads today
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Host port the portal is published on, bound to 127.0.0.1 only. The container
# always listens on 3000, which is the compiled server's own default and the
# only port number the catalog names (environment/local/config.yaml).
# SCHEMA_BASE_URL is derived from this, so changing it here is enough.
METAFRAMEWORK_HTTP_PORT=3000

# The image tag `docker compose build` produces and `up` runs. OPEN CHOICE: the
# catalog names no image, no registry and no tag scheme — both _score workload
# files say so in as many words. The default is local-only and never pushed;
# point this at a registry-qualified name once one exists, and give the Helm
# chart the same one, because 0005 is "one artifact set, two topologies".
# METAFRAMEWORK_IMAGE=metaframework-portal:dev

# The catalog to render, as a host path relative to docker/ (or absolute). The
# default is this repository's own solutions/. Any other directory holding
# solutions works, which is the no-GitHub, no-network path
# any-git-repository-is-a-catalog-source AC-1 is about — see the caveat in
# compose.yaml about it running one layer below where the requirement means it.
# METAFRAMEWORK_CATALOG=../solutions

# `working-tree` (the default) re-reads the catalog when the mount changes;
# `deployment` reads it once per process and is how you exercise what a hosted
# portal does. Nothing else is a valid value; an unrecognised one falls back to
# inferring from NODE_ENV, which in this image means `deployment`.
# METAFRAMEWORK_MODE=working-tree

# Set to 0 to drop the SSE push and the fs watcher, keeping the per-request
# fingerprint. Worth doing on macOS, where fs.watch often does not fire across a
# Docker Desktop bind mount and the push is then a cost with no effect.
# METAFRAMEWORK_LIVE_RELOAD=1

# OTLP collector endpoint, e.g. http://signoz-otel-collector:4317. Unset — the
# state this template ships in — the exporter has nowhere to send and drops,
# which is every-request-is-traced AC-5's behaviour. NOTHING IN THIS STACK READS
# IT TODAY: telemetry is `component-type: library`, `lifecycle: planned`, and no
# collector is deployed here (0004-signoz is a separate decision).
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART TWO — declared by the environment, consumed by nothing yet
#
# environment/compose/config.yaml declares these, and sources three of them from
# `env-file:docker/.env#<KEY>`. They belong to catalog-router and repo-sync,
# both `lifecycle: planned` with no code and no image, so no service in
# docker/compose.yaml consumes them and setting them changes nothing today.
#
# They are listed because the environment's configuration surface is meant to be
# reviewable in one place, and because the `env_file:` block that reads them
# should arrive in the same commit as the services that need them — not be
# invented later from memory.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# repo-sync's state directory. The one key both environments state a value for,
# and they state the same one on purpose: the two topologies differ in
# placement, not in layout (git-state-survives-a-restart AC-1).
# HUB_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/metaframework

# Cap on that directory. Optional here, REQUIRED and still unset in production.
# Setting it locally is how the eviction path gets exercised on a laptop rather
# than first in Helsinki.
# HUB_DATA_MAX_BYTES=

# A git repository on the host, mounted read-only — the no-GitHub path. Only
# meaningful here; production has no host to mount from.
# HUB_LOCAL_REPO=

# The GitHub App (0003). All optional in this environment: unset, the GitHub
# path is simply unavailable and the local mount still works. Register your own
# App against a test repository rather than borrowing another deployment's — one
# key per deployment is what revocation needs.
# GITHUB_APP_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=

# SECRET. Empty here and empty in every committed copy of this file.
# A PEM is multi-line; a .env value is not. Fold it to one line with \n escapes
# or point the App at a mounted key file when repo-sync exists to read one.
GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=

# SECRET. Identity flow only; grants no repository access.
GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=

# SECRET. Any value works locally. Sessions live in memory, so changing it or
# restarting the container signs the developer out.
HUB_SESSION_SECRET=
